WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.736
Here is a warm-up.

00:00:01.736 --> 00:00:03.730
a holds 6 * 7

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:06.601
and the third line prints the text

00:00:06.601 --> 00:00:09.712
6 * 7 equals, followed by a.

00:00:09.712 --> 00:00:10.908
The output is

00:00:10.908 --> 00:00:13.620
6 * 7 equals 42.

00:00:13.970 --> 00:00:15.750
Now the last line.

00:00:15.750 --> 00:00:19.804
It prints the text, "1 + 2 equals"

00:00:19.804 --> 00:00:22.770
then + 1, then + 2.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:24.656
What does it print?

00:00:24.656 --> 00:00:26.920
Pause the video and decide.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:33.687
Java evaluates plus from left to right.

00:00:33.687 --> 00:00:35.485
First comes a String.

00:00:35.485 --> 00:00:38.836
A String + a number is concatenation

00:00:38.836 --> 00:00:42.269
the 1 is turned into text and glued on

00:00:42.269 --> 00:00:44.884
giving "1 + 2 equals 1".

00:00:44.884 --> 00:00:46.845
That is still a String

00:00:46.845 --> 00:00:49.461
so the 2 is glued on as well.

00:00:49.461 --> 00:00:52.566
The line prints:

00:00:52.566 --> 00:00:53.220
"1 + 2 = 12".

00:00:53.570 --> 00:00:55.687
To add the numbers first

00:00:55.687 --> 00:00:57.550
put them in brackets.

00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:00.938
Now 1 + 2 is computed as an int

00:01:00.938 --> 00:01:04.410
3, and only then joined to the text.

00:01:04.990 --> 00:01:08.016
System.out.print writes its argument

00:01:08.085 --> 00:01:09.941
and stays on the same line.

00:01:09.941 --> 00:01:12.967
println writes it and then a line break

00:01:13.036 --> 00:01:14.893
so the first two calls make

00:01:14.961 --> 00:01:16.268
one line of output.

00:01:16.268 --> 00:01:18.950
The next println starts a new line.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:21.642
And printf formats numbers.

00:01:21.642 --> 00:01:24.069
%.3f means

00:01:24.069 --> 00:01:26.578
a number with three decimals

00:01:26.578 --> 00:01:29.172
and %n is a line break.

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:31.347
So pi is printed as

00:01:31.431 --> 00:01:33.020
3.142.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.817
An array is a fixed number

00:01:35.902 --> 00:01:39.653
of elements of one type, numbered from 0.

00:01:39.653 --> 00:01:43.490
Declaring the variable, `int[] someInts`

00:01:43.490 --> 00:01:46.900
creates only a reference, no array yet.

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:50.481
`new int[5]` creates the array object

00:01:50.481 --> 00:01:52.272
5 ints, all 0

00:01:52.272 --> 00:01:55.000
and `someInts` now refers to it.

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:58.595
The loop runs `i` from 0 while `i` is

00:01:58.685 --> 00:02:01.480
less than `someInts.length`.

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:04.815
`length` is a field, with no brackets

00:02:04.815 --> 00:02:06.708
and here it is 5.

00:02:06.708 --> 00:02:09.322
Each element gets `i * i`

00:02:09.322 --> 00:02:13.379
so the program prints 0, 1, 4, 9

00:02:13.379 --> 00:02:14.190
16.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:17.396
The last index is length - 1

00:02:17.396 --> 00:02:18.294
here 4.

00:02:18.294 --> 00:02:20.334
Every access is checked;

00:02:20.334 --> 00:02:23.354
index 5 would throw an exception.

00:02:23.435 --> 00:02:25.720
And the length is fixed when

00:02:25.802 --> 00:02:27.515
the array is created.

00:02:27.515 --> 00:02:29.474
Since indices are ints,

00:02:29.556 --> 00:02:32.249
no array can have more than about

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:34.860
2.1 billion elements.

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:37.661
An array of objects is an

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:39.526
array of references.

00:02:39.615 --> 00:02:42.014
new Turtle[3] creates

00:02:42.103 --> 00:02:45.389
three empty slots, each holding null,

00:02:45.389 --> 00:02:47.077
not three turtles.

00:02:47.077 --> 00:02:49.920
So element 0 prints as null.

00:02:50.270 --> 00:02:52.309
Only new Turtle()

00:02:52.388 --> 00:02:55.054
creates a turtle, on the heap,

00:02:55.054 --> 00:02:57.799
and element 0 now refers to it.

00:02:57.799 --> 00:03:00.230
Calling getX() on it gives 1.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.837
An array can also be declared

00:03:02.915 --> 00:03:05.716
and filled in one line, with braces.

00:03:05.716 --> 00:03:09.141
Primes has four elements, and the last one

00:03:09.141 --> 00:03:12.020
at index length - 1, is 7.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:14.397
A String is an object

00:03:14.397 --> 00:03:16.601
and strings are immutable;

00:03:16.601 --> 00:03:19.459
no method changes a String object.

00:03:19.459 --> 00:03:22.236
So s.concat(), on its own line,

00:03:22.236 --> 00:03:24.604
builds a new, longer string

00:03:24.604 --> 00:03:27.217
with "Data Structure" at the end,

00:03:27.217 --> 00:03:30.157
and nobody keeps a reference to it.

00:03:30.157 --> 00:03:32.280
It is simply thrown away.

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:36.478
Printing s still shows the original string.

00:03:36.478 --> 00:03:39.610
To keep the new string, assign it:

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:41.669
s = s.concat().

00:03:41.669 --> 00:03:44.532
Now s refers to the new object,

00:03:44.532 --> 00:03:48.470
and the next print shows the longer string.

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:51.187
The old string is still unchanged;

00:03:51.187 --> 00:03:52.858
with no reference left,

00:03:52.858 --> 00:03:55.364
the garbage collector will free it.

00:03:55.364 --> 00:03:58.358
Methods such as replace work the same way;

00:03:58.358 --> 00:04:00.168
they return a new string,

00:04:00.168 --> 00:04:01.700
so assign the result.

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:05.365
Here a refers to the literal "hello".

00:04:05.365 --> 00:04:08.890
And b is a new String built from "hello",

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:12.680
a second object, with the same characters.

00:04:13.030 --> 00:04:13.983
For objects,

00:04:13.983 --> 00:04:17.081
equals equals compares the references:

00:04:17.081 --> 00:04:18.908
is it the same object?

00:04:18.908 --> 00:04:21.529
These are two different objects,

00:04:21.529 --> 00:04:23.992
so a == b is false.

00:04:23.992 --> 00:04:27.407
The method equals compares the characters

00:04:27.407 --> 00:04:29.790
so a.equals(b) is true.

00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:33.012
So compare strings with equals

00:04:33.012 --> 00:04:35.420
never with ==.

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.170
Java converts a value to a

00:04:38.254 --> 00:04:40.341
wider type automatically

00:04:40.341 --> 00:04:42.762
because nothing can be lost

00:04:42.762 --> 00:04:45.350
from byte to short, int, long

00:04:45.350 --> 00:04:48.689
float and double, and from char to int.

00:04:48.689 --> 00:04:52.195
Going the other way can lose information

00:04:52.195 --> 00:04:55.200
so you must ask for it with a cast.

00:04:55.550 --> 00:04:58.679
A cast to int truncates towards zero

00:04:58.679 --> 00:05:01.808
3.99 becomes 3

00:05:01.893 --> 00:05:04.092
and -3.9

00:05:04.176 --> 00:05:06.291
9 becomes -3.

00:05:06.291 --> 00:05:08.828
To round, use Math.round

00:05:08.828 --> 00:05:10.350
which gives 4.

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:13.281
7 / 2, with two ints

00:05:13.281 --> 00:05:15.690
is integer division: 3.

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:18.271
With a double on either side

00:05:18.271 --> 00:05:21.024
the result is 3.5.

00:05:21.110 --> 00:05:23.605
Casting 7 to double first

00:05:23.691 --> 00:05:26.100
also gives 3.5.

00:05:26.450 --> 00:05:30.560
A char is a number: 'A' is 65

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:32.801
66 is 'B'

00:05:32.801 --> 00:05:35.790
and 'a' + 2 is 'c'.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:36.631
Finally,

00:05:36.724 --> 00:05:40.367
narrowing the int 200 to a byte

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:43.262
keeps only the low 8 bits

00:05:43.262 --> 00:05:44.850
-56.

00:05:45.430 --> 00:05:47.990
An array cannot change its length.

00:05:47.990 --> 00:05:50.926
Java's ArrayList is a resizable array

00:05:50.926 --> 00:05:53.184
you can keep adding elements

00:05:53.184 --> 00:05:55.367
and it makes room by itself.

00:05:55.367 --> 00:05:55.970
Inside,

00:05:55.970 --> 00:05:59.282
it keeps the elements in an ordinary array

00:05:59.282 --> 00:06:01.390
usually longer than needed.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:03.923
Add Ann, Bob and Cy

00:06:03.923 --> 00:06:08.071
they go into cells 0, 1 and 2

00:06:08.071 --> 00:06:10.145
and size is 3.

00:06:10.145 --> 00:06:12.655
Add at index 1, Dee

00:06:12.764 --> 00:06:15.602
Cy and Bob first shift one

00:06:15.711 --> 00:06:17.676
cell to the right

00:06:17.676 --> 00:06:20.841
then Dee goes into cell 1.

00:06:20.841 --> 00:06:24.989
The list prints as Ann, Dee, Bob, Cy

00:06:24.989 --> 00:06:28.700
size 4, and get(2) is Bob.

00:06:29.050 --> 00:06:32.289
When an add finds the internal array full

00:06:32.366 --> 00:06:35.373
the ArrayList creates a new array about

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:38.457
1.5 times as long and copies

00:06:38.534 --> 00:06:40.230
every element into it.

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:43.546
Copying takes time, but it happens rarely

00:06:43.623 --> 00:06:45.397
so adding at the end is

00:06:45.474 --> 00:06:47.170
still fast on average.

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:49.196
Remove by value, Bob

00:06:49.196 --> 00:06:51.990
the elements behind it shift left.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:55.422
Remove at index 0: the first name goes

00:06:55.422 --> 00:06:58.136
and two names are left, size 2.

00:06:58.216 --> 00:07:00.131
Notice that the internal

00:07:00.211 --> 00:07:03.244
array does not shrink: size goes down

00:07:03.244 --> 00:07:05.160
but the capacity stays.

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:06.637
One trap.

00:07:06.637 --> 00:07:11.120
An ArrayList of Integer, not int, holds 10,

00:07:11.120 --> 00:07:14.408
20, 30, 40 and 50.

00:07:14.408 --> 00:07:16.800
nums.remove(1)

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:20.088
an int argument means the index.

00:07:20.088 --> 00:07:24.073
So it removes the element at index 1

00:07:24.073 --> 00:07:27.660
which is 20, not the value 1.

00:07:28.010 --> 00:07:30.778
To remove a value, pass an object

00:07:30.778 --> 00:07:33.057
Integer.valueOf(40).

00:07:33.057 --> 00:07:36.720
Now 40 goes, and the list is 10, 30,

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:37.290
50.

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:40.340
Now a whole program from the slides.

00:07:40.340 --> 00:07:42.809
A text file holds pairs of numbers

00:07:42.809 --> 00:07:44.112
one pair per line

00:07:44.181 --> 00:07:46.102
and we write the sum of each

00:07:46.170 --> 00:07:47.611
pair to another file.

00:07:47.611 --> 00:07:50.286
A BufferedReader reads the input file

00:07:50.286 --> 00:07:53.030
and a BufferedWriter writes the output.

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:55.357
readLine returns one line

00:07:55.433 --> 00:07:58.475
without its line break: "1 3".

00:07:58.475 --> 00:08:01.289
Split at the space gives two pieces

00:08:01.289 --> 00:08:03.874
parseInt turns each into an int

00:08:03.874 --> 00:08:06.840
and the writer writes their sum, 4,

00:08:06.840 --> 00:08:08.057
and a new line.

00:08:08.057 --> 00:08:11.099
The next line, 2 and 4, gives 6,

00:08:11.099 --> 00:08:13.380
and 5 and 6 give 11.

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:15.426
At the end of the file

00:08:15.426 --> 00:08:18.670
readLine returns null, and the loop stops

00:08:18.670 --> 00:08:19.407
it reads,

00:08:19.407 --> 00:08:22.061
assigns and tests in one condition.

00:08:22.061 --> 00:08:24.052
Finally, close the writer.

00:08:24.125 --> 00:08:25.969
A BufferedWriter collects

00:08:26.042 --> 00:08:27.591
its output in memory

00:08:27.664 --> 00:08:30.023
and closing it writes everything

00:08:30.097 --> 00:08:30.982
to the file.

00:08:31.056 --> 00:08:33.857
Forgetting to close is the most common

00:08:33.931 --> 00:08:36.290
reason for an empty output file.

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:40.172
Java reads and writes files as streams.

00:08:40.257 --> 00:08:42.797
A FileInputStream and a

00:08:42.881 --> 00:08:45.337
FileOutputStream move raw bytes

00:08:45.337 --> 00:08:48.046
for binary data such as images.

00:08:48.046 --> 00:08:50.925
Their read returns the next byte

00:08:50.925 --> 00:08:54.057
from 0 to 255

00:08:54.057 --> 00:08:57.190
or -1 at the end of the file.

00:08:57.540 --> 00:09:00.276
For text, use the character streams

00:09:00.276 --> 00:09:02.328
FileReader and FileWriter

00:09:02.404 --> 00:09:04.988
and wrap a BufferedReader around a

00:09:05.064 --> 00:09:07.420
FileReader to read whole lines.

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:09.430
Whatever you open, close.

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:10.360
Better still,

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:13.214
open it in a try-with-resources statement

00:09:13.214 --> 00:09:15.273
which closes it automatically

00:09:15.273 --> 00:09:17.530
even when an exception is thrown.

00:09:18.110 --> 00:09:18.862
To sum up.

00:09:18.862 --> 00:09:21.417
One: an array has a fixed length

00:09:21.492 --> 00:09:23.672
and its indices run from 0

00:09:23.747 --> 00:09:25.251
to length - 1.

00:09:25.251 --> 00:09:27.355
Two: strings are immutable,

00:09:27.355 --> 00:09:30.587
so keep the result of every string method

00:09:30.587 --> 00:09:33.068
and compare strings with equals.

00:09:33.068 --> 00:09:35.999
Three: narrowing a value needs a cast,

00:09:36.074 --> 00:09:38.855
and an ArrayList grows by copying its

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:41.110
elements into a bigger array.

00:09:41.460 --> 00:09:42.869
Try this yourself.

00:09:42.869 --> 00:09:45.294
Write a method, reverseWords,

00:09:45.294 --> 00:09:48.503
that turns "data structures are fun" into

00:09:48.503 --> 00:09:50.459
"fun are structures data".

00:09:50.459 --> 00:09:52.963
Split the string at the spaces

00:09:53.041 --> 00:09:55.311
and build the result from the

00:09:55.389 --> 00:09:57.580
last word back to the first.

00:09:57.930 --> 00:09:59.087
In Lecture 3,

00:09:59.087 --> 00:10:01.266
we look at arrays more closely:

00:10:01.266 --> 00:10:02.900
how they sit in memory

00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:05.010
and what each operation costs.

